WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
[SOUND]

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Most apps keep track of pieces of information that change and

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:09.983
update as you use the app.

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:15.703
For example, a simple game can keep track of your score and the number of lives you have left as you play.

00:00:16.303 --> 00:00:19.967
Apps utilize the computer's memory to do this, and learning how to use it

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:24.367
use that memory when programming is a really powerful skill.

00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:29.951
The most common way to leverage a computer's memory is by creating a variable.

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:33.591
is just a container for storing a value.

00:00:33.591 --> 00:00:37.511
And once created, the variable is stored in the computer's memory,

00:00:37.511 --> 00:00:39.911
and it can be used and updated.

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:42.735
repeatedly throughout your program.

00:00:42.735 --> 00:00:47.775
As an example, let's use variables to keep track of the score and the number of lives

00:00:47.775 --> 00:00:49.455
remaining in a hypothetical game.

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:52.519
Creating the variable is really easy.

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:57.439
You use the word var followed by a label that you want to give the variable.

00:00:57.439 --> 00:00:59.903
You can choose any label you want, but it's not.

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:02.823
It helps to choose a word that describes the variable

00:01:02.823 --> 00:01:04.503
that you want to store.

00:01:04.503 --> 00:01:07.023
So let's label our first variable score

00:01:07.023 --> 00:01:08.823
to keep track of the score.

00:01:08.823 --> 00:01:09.887
And then we can create a--

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:13.667
second variable to keep track of lives.

00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:15.447
Remember these labels.

00:01:15.447 --> 00:01:19.287
You'll need them later in order to use the variables.

00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:19.871
We can assign--

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:25.271
a value to the variable using the equal sign, like this.

00:01:25.271 --> 00:01:29.591
Let's set our starting score to zero and our number of lives to three.

00:01:29.591 --> 00:01:29.855
Remember you can do this.

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:34.455
you only need to use the var at the time that you want to create a new variable. A common

00:01:34.455 --> 00:01:39.735
mistake is to use it every single time you want to refer to a variable. Also, be sure

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:45.079
avoid mixing up the order when you call your variables. The labels have to appear on the

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:48.959
left and the value has to appear on the right. Like this.

00:01:49.823 --> 00:01:51.823
Not like this.

00:01:51.823 --> 00:01:54.343
Often, you'll want to create a variable and assign it a

00:01:54.343 --> 00:01:55.783
value right away.

00:01:55.783 --> 00:01:58.183
There's a really convenient way to do this with one line of

00:01:58.183 --> 00:01:59.263
code.

00:01:59.263 --> 00:01:59.807
This is sometimes--

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:02.367
is called initializing the variable.

00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:05.327
It's important to realize that this single line of code

00:02:05.327 --> 00:02:07.287
is actually doing two things.

00:02:07.287 --> 00:02:09.791
First, creating the variable, and then second--

00:02:09.791 --> 00:02:11.791
assigning it a value.

00:02:12.351 --> 00:02:19.471
Let's take a moment to talk about the equal sign. In many coding language, the equal symbol is called the assignment operator.

00:02:19.775 --> 00:02:22.775
because you use it to assign a value to a variable.

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:24.775
And you're probably used to seeing it in math,

00:02:24.775 --> 00:02:27.775
but in programming it's a little bit different.

00:02:27.775 --> 00:02:29.759
If you were in math class, and you saw...

00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:36.439
saw this, something would definitely be wrong. Score can't possibly equal both 5 and 7,

00:02:36.439 --> 00:02:39.719
but this is not math class.

00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:45.583
The computer is executing a set of instructions one after the other, so this would just tell

00:02:45.583 --> 00:02:49.703
the computer to set score to 5, and then on the next line, set it to 5.

00:02:49.727 --> 00:02:51.527
to 7.

00:02:51.527 --> 00:02:55.507
In an effort to avoid confusion, programmers often don't use the word "equals" when

00:02:55.507 --> 00:02:56.887
we see this sign.

00:02:56.887 --> 00:02:59.687
Instead, they use the phrase "gets the value."

00:02:59.711 --> 00:03:06.351
value. So when we see a statement like this, we could read it as score gets the value 7,

00:03:06.351 --> 00:03:09.695
or score gets 7 for short. Some programming language.

00:03:09.695 --> 00:03:14.215
which is to avoid the confusion entirely by representing the assignment statements with

00:03:14.215 --> 00:03:17.615
a sideways arrow, like this.

00:03:17.615 --> 00:03:19.679
To use a variable, you just

00:03:19.679 --> 00:03:25.079
refer to it by its label. Anywhere the variable name appears in the program, the value currently

00:03:25.079 --> 00:03:28.599
stored in the variable will be retrieved from memory.

00:03:28.599 --> 00:03:29.639
The tricky part is

00:03:29.663 --> 00:03:35.503
is that when using a variable, you often can't see visually what its value is.

00:03:35.503 --> 00:03:39.623
To bypass that problem, you can use write or code.

00:03:39.647 --> 00:03:43.487
console.log to display the value like this.

00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:47.047
This is a really handy trick,

00:03:47.047 --> 00:03:49.631
but remember there's a difference between showing.

00:03:49.631 --> 00:03:54.211
the value of a variable and writing out the variable's label.

00:03:54.211 --> 00:03:58.331
When text appears in quotes, you're telling the computer to write those literal ASCII

00:03:58.331 --> 00:03:59.551
characters on the screen

00:03:59.615 --> 00:04:01.615
like this.

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:15.199
retrieve its value. If you haven't made that variable, then the computer will give an error.

00:04:19.583 --> 00:04:21.663
(chimes)
